==English==

===Etymology===
When used idiomatically, probably an [[allusion]] to a [[police officer]] blowing his or her whistle on observing a violation of the law.

===Verb===
{{en-verb|blows the whistle|blowing the whistle|blew the whistle|blown the whistle}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic|usually|with "on"}} To [[disclose]] [[information]] to the [[public]] or to [[appropriate]] [[authority|authorities]] concerning the [[illegal]] or [[socially]] [[harmful]] actions of a person or group, especially a [[corporation]] or [[government]] [[agency]].
#: ''In some jurisdictions, it is illegal to fire a person for '''blowing the whistle''' on an employer.''
# {{lb|en|team sports}} To make a piercing sound which [[signal]]s a [[referee]]'s action or the end of a game.

